#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180501} :activity/id #uuid "912b55f4-8877-5aff-bc43-d73698914e66" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049346 :topic/id #uuid "ed9d339c-fde4-545b-86b4-381d2f85203f" :topic/math-academy-id 3704} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180386 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180678 :tutorial/content "Since there is a fundamental link between line integrals of vector-valued functions and line integrals with respect to $x$ and $y,$ it should be unsurprising that they share similar properties. We list two of them below:\n\n- If $-C$ denotes the path obtained by reversing the orientation of $C,$ then\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{-C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r = -\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r.\n$$\n\n- If $C = C_1\\cup C_2$ is the union of two paths $C_1$ and $C_2$, then\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r \n= \n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r + \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r.\n$$\n\nThe first property has an intuitive meaning if we think about it in terms of the work done by a force on a particle. To see this, consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ shown below. Let $C$ be the path defined by traversing the line segment from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/4f/4f69f80d98d301410908f7ec867a89bdc834c22a51f224d9b00b80ceca39ab87.png)\n\n \n\nLet $W(C)$ be the work done in moving the particle along $C.$ Since our particle moves in the same direction as the vector field, we have\n\n$$\nW(C) = \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r > 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if we move the particle from $B$ to $A,$ we expect the line integral to be negative. This is because the particle is now moving against $\\mathbf F.$ \n\n![](images/fe/fe6b2e67d33a4993104406c88b1dc0dc897be33d034007ed581a75f741ddf620.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW(-C) = \\int\\limits_{-C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r < 0.\n$$\n\nWe can show that the work done in moving the particle along $-C$ is the same as the work done in moving it along $C$ but with the opposite sign. That is\n\n$$\nW(-C) = -W(C).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{-C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r = -\\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d745eed3-7e69-55af-b685-1c7482dfffb9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3976 :tutorial/title "Reversing the Orientation of a Curve and Sums Over Unions of Paths"} :step/id #uuid "b7ff2ff0-c95d-5155-89fb-bfd7eb079783" :step/math-academy-id 23622 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180390}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180387 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179669 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179524 :question/id #uuid "734d74ca-26cf-57d3-b3dc-fec66d7e0264" :question/math-academy-id "e-6286"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5ce5418-6b75-5efa-b7f9-99ee16c510be" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048916}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345285} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415774}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Line Integral of a Vector-Valued Function Along a Union of Curves"} :step/id #uuid "5214fa4f-2dd0-5f35-827a-aa639d600260" :step/math-academy-id 23623} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180390 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179672 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179527 :question/id #uuid "2e6103cc-7be6-5eb6-ab5a-aa5aff3fa423" :question/math-academy-id "e-6319"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70b4357f-d5c5-5946-9022-0602c4217a1d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050687}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415772}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Properties of Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions"} :step/id #uuid "6acb0f4b-5b56-5e4c-a728-4db2fd840bff" :step/math-academy-id 23747 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180387}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180492 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179738 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179600 :question/id #uuid "171fef73-d124-5aca-ad06-9f20ae5a087c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9535"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bed301c-59ac-5cb3-85e4-4aeaeebc15c2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050666}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415775}] :knowledge-point/title "Establishing the Connection With Line Integrals of Scalar Fields"} :step/id #uuid "c7465601-d18a-5a90-bfe9-a27fe52c871f" :step/math-academy-id 35726 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180386}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180501 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180722 :tutorial/content "Line integrals of vector-valued functions share a fundamental connection with line integrals with respect to $x$ and $y.$\n\nConsider a vector field $\\mathbf F$ on $\\mathbb R^2$ with components $P$ and $Q\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nNow suppose that $C$ is a smooth curve on $\\mathbb R^2,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r (t) = x(t)\\,\\mathbf i + y(t)\\,\\mathbf j, \\qquad t\\in [a,b].\n$$\n\nThen, we can write the line integral of $\\mathbf F$ along $C$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}(P(x(t),y(t))\\,i+Q(x(t),y(t))\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}(P(x(t),y(t))\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+Q(x(t),y(t))\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}P(x(t),y(t))\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+Q(x(t),y(t))\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{C}P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r  = \\int\\limits_C P(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x + Q(x,y)  \\,\\textrm d y\n$$\n\nAn analogous result holds for vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3.$ That is, \n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r  = \\int\\limits_C P(x,y,z)\\,\\textrm d x + Q(x,y,z)  \\,\\textrm d y + R(x,y,z) \\,\\textrm d z\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = P(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf j + R(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf k.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b4ca8cae-81df-5c55-90ac-839b063e78a6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5837 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4246c091-bba6-587e-8d43-72e5c01a1e08" :step/math-academy-id 35769 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180492}}] :activity/title "Properties of Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179857}]}
